# Line endings, decided here rather than by whichever machine cloned.
#
# Git for Windows installs with `core.autocrlf=true`, which converts every
# text file to CRLF on checkout. The Windows workflow found what that does
# here on its first run: the golden fixtures came off the runner with CRLF
# and every golden failed against files git had rewritten, and a `#!/bin/sh`
# script under `packaging/` with CRLF endings fails on Linux naming an
# interpreter that does not exist. `text=auto eol=lf` normalizes to LF in the
# repository and checks out LF everywhere. slipcase-desktop's file, from
# which this is taken, records the shell-script half.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Templates that become a Debian control file, a property list and a
# package manifest, all line-oriented where CRLF is not part of the format.
*.in text eol=lf

# The toml-test corpus is byte-exact by definition: nine of its cases carry
# a carriage return on purpose, valid and invalid, and `tests/roundtrip.rs`
# compares what a save writes against the bytes as vendored. No conversion
# in either direction.
flyleaf-core/tests/toml-test/** -text

# Not text, whatever the contents look like to a heuristic.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.deb binary
*.wasm binary
